  "brief_md": "# Evidence Brief: O03 — Automated ADA/WCAG Audit Platform for Municipal Governments\n\n## Regulatory context\nThe DOJ's 2024 Title II final rule requires state/local government websites and mobile apps to meet WCAG 2.1 AA. Critically, in **April 2026 the DOJ issued an Interim Final Rule extending the deadline**: large entities (population 50,000+) now have until **April 26, 2027** (was April 24, 2026), and smaller entities until **April 26, 2028** (source: ada.gov, Federal Register, NCLM). Several competitor marketing sites (Quillinox, GovAccess, AccessScan, ADA Compliant) still display the original April 2026 date, suggesting stale urgency messaging across the space.\n\n## Payer\nThe buyer is a municipal government — typically an IT director, webmaster, communications officer, or city clerk who can approve a purchase under the federal/state micro-purchase threshold (~$10,000) without triggering an RFP. Comparable published price: RatedWithAI's $29/mo ($249/yr) Starter plan, explicitly marketed as staying under micro-purchase thresholds (https://ratedwithai.com/government).\n\n## Competitors\nAt least seven direct or adjacent competitors were found, all live and marketing against this exact deadline:\n- ADA Compliant — $29/mo Pro (https://adacompliant.io/government)\n- RatedWithAI — $29–79/mo, .gov discount (https://ratedwithai.com/government)\n- GovAccess — $149/mo Pro, $349/mo Agency (https://govaccess.us/)\n- AccessScan/doaccessscan.com — $49–99 one-time report (https://doaccessscan.com/)\n- Quillinox — enterprise, demo-gated, no public price, more full-featured (scan + PDF remediation + grievance tracking + cryptographic audit trail) (https://quillinox.com/)\n- OnePageAudit — $49/mo monitoring product line, general market with a government landing page (https://onepageaudit.com/government)\n- CivicPlus (AudioEye/DocAccess) — adjacent incumbent CMS vendor upsell, enterprise pricing not published (https://www.civicplus.com/web-accessibility-software/)\n\nAll of the pure-play entrants use a near-identical playbook: free single-page scan → plain-English/PDF report → low monthly or one-time fee pitched as \"under the micro-purchase threshold, no RFP needed.\"\n\n## Acquisition channel\n- State municipal league conference exhibit booths run **$1,300–$1,800** per 10x10 booth (Maryland Municipal League, https://exhibits.mdmunicipal.org/2026/).\n- State league sponsorship/advertising packages range **$250–$7,500** (NCLM, https://www.nclm.org/about-us/partners-sponsors/).\nNo CPC/paid-search cost data was found for \"ADA compliance\" government keywords in this pass.\n\n## Demand evidence\n- Mike Teeters, senior product manager at Tyler Technologies, on the deadline: *\"There will be challenges in meeting that deadline across the board because of legacy systems and funding.\"* (https://www.govtech.com/biz/challenges-for-government-as-ada-compliance-deadline-nears)\n- RatedWithAI's marketing copy states: *\"Small cities across America are deleting website content rather than paying for expensive compliance tools.\"* (https://ratedwithai.com/government) — vendor-sourced, not an independent quote, included as directional evidence only.\n- NLC (National League of Cities) is actively surveying member cities on estimated compliance costs, indicating real uncertainty and unmet need among municipal staff (https://www.nlc.org/article/2026/02/18/is-your-city-ready-for-website-accessibility-requirements/).\n\n## Case for\nThe regulatory trigger is legally unambiguous and the buyer universe is enumerable; a solo builder using AI agents can assemble a scan-plus-report SaaS from commodity WCAG-checking libraries (axe-core) in weeks, and multiple existing single/small-team competitors already prove a self-serve, no-RFP, $29-149/mo pricing model can close government purchases without an enterprise sales team.\n\n## Case against\nThe niche is already crowded with at least six to seven nearly identical automated-scan competitors that launched in the same window, all using the same \"free scan → PDF report → under-micro-purchase-threshold subscription\" playbook — indicating the wedge is commoditized and a price war is already underway. The April 2026 deadline extension to 2027/2028 undercuts the urgency narrative most of these tools (and this idea) are built on. Automated scanning alone is explicitly insufficient for real compliance per DOJ guidance and case law on overlays, creating both a ceiling on product value and liability exposure if a report is oversold as \"compliant.\" Government sales cycles and procurement/vendor-onboarding friction (W-9s, insurance, budget-cycle timing) remain real even at low price points.\n\n## Open questions for a market test\n- Does purchase urgency survive the 2027/2028 deadline extension?\n- Can a new entrant differentiate against 6+ near-identical existing tools without a large marketing budget?\n- What is the real self-serve conversion rate from free scan to paid, without human sales involvement?\n- How many of the ~90,000 entities have any web complexity worth auditing vs. a single static page?\n- Does automated-only scanning expose the vendor to liability if a report is later contradicted in litigation?\n- Is there a defensible deeper wedge (e.g., PDF remediation, grievance tracking, audit trails as Quillinox is attempting) buildable in six weeks by one person with agents, given full compliance requires manual assistive-technology testing that automation cannot replace?"
